0c7b6-69 | 2026-06-12T11:15:40Z | session:0c7b6c09-7d1f-4cb2-b178-1adf69cd09ef | NMP (the Rust app-facade pattern — fire-and-forget action dispatch, bounded state snapshots pushed to the platform via a reconciler, documented in `docs/wiki/nmp-app-facade.md`) is effectively at 100% where it applies: \n  \n - **Rust core** (`app/core/src/nmp_app.rs` + `nmp_runtime.rs`): all business logic and state lives here. \n - **iOS**: yesterday's migration commit deleted the 7 feature-scoped stores (CommentsStore, ChatStore, RoomStore, FeedbackStore, etc.) plus legacy wrappers like `SafeHighlighterCore`. Everything now reads from a single `nmpState` on `HighlighterStore` and dispatches via `nmpApp.dispatch(...)`. The only Swift-side stores left are `PodcastPlayerStore` (AVPlayer position) and `CaptureStore` (local OCR pipeline) — transient device-local state that legitimately doesn't belong in the Rust core. No migration TODOs remain. \n - **Android**: built NMP-native from the start, single `HighlighterViewModel` exposing the reconciled state as a `StateFlow`. \n - **Web** (`web/`, SvelteKit): uses NDK directly and has no NMP integration. That looks like a deliberate architectural boundary rather than unfinished migration, but it does mean the web app duplicates logic the Rust core owns — worth deciding explicitly whether that stays permanent (e.g., via a WASM build of the core) or not. [...] **"Doesn't connect to relays" — root cause found.** The Android app only wired 1 of 3 required NMP channels. It listens for state snapshots but never registers the core event bridge (`setCoreEventCallback`) that iOS registers before bootstrap — so relay status changes, NIP-46 login completion, and all live data deltas were being **silently dropped**. The sockets actually open; the app just never hears about it ("Ready" = `Unknown`, forever). On top of that, session credentials were never persisted (empty stubs), so every launch started logged out. \n  [...] - **Observability fixed at the root**: the Rust core's `tracing` output was dropped on *both* platforms (no subscriber ever installed). New `initPlatformLogging()` export routes it to logcat on Android and stderr/Xcode console on iOS. iOS is wired and building now; the rebuild agent was told to wire the Android side. \n - **iOS accessibility/logging pass landed**: 32 VoiceOver labels across 20 files, 11 silent `try?` failures now logged with context — build + 22/22 tests green. [...] - **Root cause of "never connects" confirmed and fixed:** Android never called `setCoreEventCallback`, so `SignerConnected`/relay deltas were dropped. I register a Kotlin `EventBridge` in the VM **before** dispatching `Bootstrap`, mirroring iOS `registerEventBridge()`. \n - **Persistence implemented:** `onPersistSessionCredential`/`onClearSessionCredentials` now write/clear the encrypted store. `bootstrap()` restores the stored credential and dispatches `SignInNsec(persist=false, clearStoredOnFailure=true)` / `PairBunker(...)` — exactly iOS `dispatchStoredCredential`. Fresh UI logins use `persist=true, clearStoredOnFailure=false`. [...] The top-bar status line reads **"Ready"** (maps from `HighlighterConnectionState.UNKNOWN`) and did **not** advance to "Online" even though data flowed (24 rooms, 89 highlights, `relays=4` applied). The core emits no connection-state transition / `RelayStatusChanged`-driven chrome recompute at the level the chrome snapshot reads — this is core-side behavior, not the Android wiring (iOS's `EventBridge` also no-ops on `relayStatusChanged`, and the brief forbids touching `app/core`). Connectivity itself is proven working by the loaded network data. \n  [...] - **Relay connection status** — root cause was in the Rust core, not just Android: the kernel's built-in relay-diagnostics projection only travels inside emitted snapshot frames, which highlighter-core never decoded. Wired `nmp_app_set_update_callback` → frame decode → diagnostics state. Status line now reads **Online**, and this also fixes iOS's relay rows (same starved code path). \n - **Relay management UI** — Settings → Network now lists all configured relays with live status dots and roles (4 connected), auto-connected relays, plus add/remove. Screenshot-verified.
