0c7b6-82 | 2026-06-12T11:34:01Z | session:0c7b6c09-7d1f-4cb2-b178-1adf69cd09ef | **"Doesn't connect to relays" — root cause found.** The Android app only wired 1 of 3 required NMP channels. It listens for state snapshots but never registers the core event bridge (`setCoreEventCallback`) that iOS registers before bootstrap — so relay status changes, NIP-46 login completion, and all live data deltas were being **silently dropped**. The sockets actually open; the app just never hears about it ("Ready" = `Unknown`, forever). On top of that, session credentials were never persisted (empty stubs), so every launch started logged out. \n  [...] - **Root cause of "never connects" confirmed and fixed:** Android never called `setCoreEventCallback`, so `SignerConnected`/relay deltas were dropped. I register a Kotlin `EventBridge` in the VM **before** dispatching `Bootstrap`, mirroring iOS `registerEventBridge()`. \n - **Persistence implemented:** `onPersistSessionCredential`/`onClearSessionCredentials` now write/clear the encrypted store. `bootstrap()` restores the stored credential and dispatches `SignInNsec(persist=false, clearStoredOnFailure=true)` / `PairBunker(...)` — exactly iOS `dispatchStoredCredential`. Fresh UI logins use `persist=true, clearStoredOnFailure=false`. [...] - **Relay connection status** — root cause was in the Rust core, not just Android: the kernel's built-in relay-diagnostics projection only travels inside emitted snapshot frames, which highlighter-core never decoded. Wired `nmp_app_set_update_callback` → frame decode → diagnostics state. Status line now reads **Online**, and this also fixes iOS's relay rows (same starved code path). \n - **Relay management UI** — Settings → Network now lists all configured relays with live status dots and roles (4 connected), auto-connected relays, plus add/remove. Screenshot-verified.
