0c7b6-117 | 2026-06-12T14:07:04Z | session:0c7b6c09-7d1f-4cb2-b178-1adf69cd09ef | Assistant: **The app — specifically `highlighter-core` (`app/core` in this repo), not the NMP framework.** \n  \n The evidence from the research: \n  \n - **NMP itself is clean and explicit about this.** Its doctrine (D3/D8) forbids blocking the actor thread, and when the framework found the same bug class *inside its own kernel* (V-90), it fixed it with ADR-0040 (accepted 2026-05-31) using a serialized worker + parked-operation pattern. The framework also already ships the right primitives: correlation-ID action results, capability workers, push-based deltas. \n  \n - **The violation is in this repo's facade.** `app/core/src/nmp_app.rs` runs its own single-threaded actor and calls `runtime.block_on(...)` on network-dependent NMP operations *from inside that loop* — waiting up to 360s for a relay ACK (uploads, chat publish, room join) or 65s per signature. Even the timeout constants live here, in `app/core/src/nmp_runtime.rs`, not in NMP. \n  \n - The ironic confirmation: this same app already implements the correct non-blocking pattern in nine bespoke worker threads (account creation, search, bunker sign-in…) — it just didn't apply it consistently to the other ~30 network paths. \n  [...] 2. **Framework intent is unambiguous.** The NMP repo's doctrine (D3/D8) forbids actor stalls; ADR-0024 prescribes the two-phase correlation-ID re-entry protocol; **ADR-0040 (Accepted 2026-05-31) closed the identical bug class (V-90) in nmp-core itself** with a serialized capability worker + `PendingSign` parked-op pattern; ADR-0031 is the worker-feeds-actor precedent. The framework explicitly provides the right primitive — highlighter's facade violates it, while ironically already implementing it correctly in nine bespoke workers (account-create, bunker sign-in, search, etc.). \n  [...]  \n **The framework already prescribes the answer.** The NMP repo's own doctrine forbids exactly this (D3/D8 non-blocking contracts), and its **ADR-0040 fixed the identical bug class inside nmp-core itself** a couple of weeks ago using a serialized worker + parked-operation pattern. Highlighter's facade violates the doctrine — while ironically already implementing the correct pattern in nine bespoke worker threads (account-create, search, bunker sign-in…). \n  [...] 1. **Blocking surface (92 sites, re-classified).** The naive "every refresh hits the network" reading is wrong: most `get_*`/`subscribe_*` core methods are pure nostrdb reads or sync interest registration (e.g. `client.rs:473`, `:542`, `:1514`, `:236`). The real wedges fall into three classes: \n    - **Class A — 360 s waits (5 sites):** `dispatch_action_for_result` waits on a correlation row with `NMP_PROTOCOL_ACTION_TIMEOUT = 360 s` (`nmp_runtime.rs:56`, `:774`). In-actor sites: blossom uploads (`blossom.rs:247` via nmp_app.rs:2288/2656/2976), NIP-29 chat publish (`chat.rs:101` via 3264), room join (`groups.rs:220` via 2911). Six-minute actor freeze each. \n    - **Class B — 65 s sign waits (~24 sites):** every publish helper signs through `NmpNostrSigner::sign_event` → `sign_unsigned_event` with `NMP_SIGN_TIMEOUT = 65 s` (`nmp_runtime.rs:54`, `:651`). Microseconds with a local key, up to 65 s per signature with a NIP-46 bunker — the exact V-90 class nmp-core fixed for itself. \n    - **Class C — 4–6 s bounded HTTP/fetch (3 sites):** NIP-11 probe, relay-list import, entity resolve. Survivable individually but stalls are serial per message. \n    - **Class D — ~58 local-only sites:** keep synchronous. \n
0c7b6-118 | 2026-06-12T14:07:04Z | session:0c7b6c09-7d1f-4cb2-b178-1adf69cd09ef | RESULTS — chosen strategy: **hybrid (d)**, formalized as one `OpRunner` primitive: shared 2-thread tokio runtime, `OpDomain`-keyed in-flight registry with generation supersession + `AbortHandle`, a single `KernelMsg::OpResolved` variant, all state mutation staying on the actor thread (`apply_op_outcome`), 30 s worker-side deadlines, D6 toast/busy-flag error surfacing. Rejected: timeouts-only (proven insufficient by the 30 s account-create repro), and concurrent async handlers (breaks the exclusive `state.write()`-between-emits invariant). \n  [...] **Recommended fix: one `OpRunner` primitive** that generalizes those nine workers — a shared 2-thread tokio runtime, a domain-keyed in-flight registry with generation-based supersession (tap refresh twice → first op aborts) and `AbortHandle` cancellation, a single `KernelMsg::OpResolved` message back to the actor, and all state mutation staying on the actor thread so the exclusive-write-between-emits invariant survives. Timeouts-only was rejected (our 30s account-create timeout already proved insufficient on-device); fully concurrent async handlers were rejected (breaks state consistency). \n
0c7b6-119 | 2026-06-12T14:07:04Z | session:0c7b6c09-7d1f-4cb2-b178-1adf69cd09ef |  \n Assistant: The design doc is now at `docs/architecture/actor-blocking-fix.md` (241 lines). The short version: \n  [...] NEXT — migration phases in the doc: Phase 0 (stall instrumentation + `block_on_local` wrapper + CI lint, 1–2 days), Phase 1 (top-10 wedge sites incl. all Class A, 4–6 days), Phase 2 (~24 publish helpers, 1.5–2 weeks), Phase 3 (`handle_core_delta` hardening), Phase 4 (fold nine legacy workers into `OpRunner`). Test strategy includes a deterministic dead-network harness — relay URLs are currently compile-time baked via `include_str!("relay_policy.json")` (`relays.rs:46-56`), so the design adds a `relay_policy_json` config seam plus a black-hole TCP listener and the framework's `nak serve` in-process relay precedent, with an account-creation regression test that fails on today's code by construction. \n
