0c7b6-130 | 2026-06-12T15:05:29Z | session:0c7b6c09-7d1f-4cb2-b178-1adf69cd09ef | Assistant: **The app — specifically `highlighter-core` (`app/core` in this repo), not the NMP framework.** \n  \n The evidence from the research: \n  \n - **NMP itself is clean and explicit about this.** Its doctrine (D3/D8) forbids blocking the actor thread, and when the framework found the same bug class *inside its own kernel* (V-90), it fixed it with ADR-0040 (accepted 2026-05-31) using a serialized worker + parked-operation pattern. The framework also already ships the right primitives: correlation-ID action results, capability workers, push-based deltas. \n  \n - **The violation is in this repo's facade.** `app/core/src/nmp_app.rs` runs its own single-threaded actor and calls `runtime.block_on(...)` on network-dependent NMP operations *from inside that loop* — waiting up to 360s for a relay ACK (uploads, chat publish, room join) or 65s per signature. Even the timeout constants live here, in `app/core/src/nmp_runtime.rs`, not in NMP. \n  \n - The ironic confirmation: this same app already implements the correct non-blocking pattern in nine bespoke worker threads (account creation, search, bunker sign-in…) — it just didn't apply it consistently to the other ~30 network paths. \n  \n One small caveat: NMP's APIs make the mistake *easy* — `dispatch_nmp_action_for_result` hands you a future that can pend for six minutes, and nothing stops you awaiting it on your actor thread. A framework-side guardrail (e.g. a debug assertion or a callback-style API) could prevent the next app from repeating this. But the defect to fix is in this repo, and the fix (the `OpRunner` design) requires **zero changes to nostr-multi-platform**. [...]    - **Class A — 360 s waits (5 sites):** `dispatch_action_for_result` waits on a correlation row with `NMP_PROTOCOL_ACTION_TIMEOUT = 360 s` (`nmp_runtime.rs:56`, `:774`). In-actor sites: blossom uploads (`blossom.rs:247` via nmp_app.rs:2288/2656/2976), NIP-29 chat publish (`chat.rs:101` via 3264), room join (`groups.rs:220` via 2911). Six-minute actor freeze each. \n    - **Class B — 65 s sign waits (~24 sites):** every publish helper signs through `NmpNostrSigner::sign_event` → `sign_unsigned_event` with `NMP_SIGN_TIMEOUT = 65 s` (`nmp_runtime.rs:54`, `:651`). Microseconds with a local key, up to 65 s per signature with a NIP-46 bunker — the exact V-90 class nmp-core fixed for itself. \n    - **Class C — 4–6 s bounded HTTP/fetch (3 sites):** NIP-11 probe, relay-list import, entity resolve. Survivable individually but stalls are serial per message. \n    - **Class D — ~58 local-only sites:** keep synchronous. \n  [...] - **Class A (5 sites, 360-second waits):** Blossom photo uploads, chat-message publish, and room-join all wait on a relay ACK with a 6-minute protocol timeout — each one freezes the entire actor (and all UI snapshots) for up to six minutes on a dead network. \n - **Class B (~24 sites, 65-second waits):** every publish helper signs through the NMP signer with a 65s timeout — instant with a local key, but up to 65s per signature with a NIP-46 bunker signer. \n - **Class C (3 sites):** bounded 4–6s HTTP fetches; tolerable but serial. \n
0c7b6-131 | 2026-06-12T15:05:29Z | session:0c7b6c09-7d1f-4cb2-b178-1adf69cd09ef | RESULTS — chosen strategy: **hybrid (d)**, formalized as one `OpRunner` primitive: shared 2-thread tokio runtime, `OpDomain`-keyed in-flight registry with generation supersession + `AbortHandle`, a single `KernelMsg::OpResolved` variant, all state mutation staying on the actor thread (`apply_op_outcome`), 30 s worker-side deadlines, D6 toast/busy-flag error surfacing. Rejected: timeouts-only (proven insufficient by the 30 s account-create repro), and concurrent async handlers (breaks the exclusive `state.write()`-between-emits invariant). [...] **Recommended fix: one `OpRunner` primitive** that generalizes those nine workers — a shared 2-thread tokio runtime, a domain-keyed in-flight registry with generation-based supersession (tap refresh twice → first op aborts) and `AbortHandle` cancellation, a single `KernelMsg::OpResolved` message back to the actor, and all state mutation staying on the actor thread so the exclusive-write-between-emits invariant survives. Timeouts-only was rejected (our 30s account-create timeout already proved insufficient on-device); fully concurrent async handlers were rejected (breaks state consistency). \n  [...] - **`OpRunner`** (in `ActorRuntimes`, the actor's `&mut` state — `ActorContext` is `&`-shared so `submit_op` couldn't live there): 2-worker `new_multi_thread` runtime, `in_flight: HashMap<OpDomain, InFlightOp>` (AbortHandle + started), `generations: HashMap<OpDomain, u64>`, `submit_op` (bump generation → abort prior → worker-side `tokio::time::timeout` → `KernelMsg::OpResolved`), `is_stale`, and `cancel_all`. `OpDomain`, `OpOutcome` (per-domain typed payloads mirroring today's results), single `KernelMsg::OpResolved { domain, generation, outcome }`, and `apply_op_outcome` as the only state-mutation point with the staleness check. \n - **Logout arm** calls `ops.cancel_all()`; the `Stop` arm also calls it. `pending_joins` bookkeeping moved into `apply_op_outcome` for `JoinRoom`. [...] **None.** Every migrated domain reused an existing busy flag (`is_cover_uploading`, `is_creating`, `is_sending_chat_message`, `is_picture/banner_uploading`, `is_uploading`, `is_minting_invite_link`, `is_fetching`, nip11 `is_loading`, `pending_joins`). The only additive uniffi change is `HighlighterAppConfig.relay_policy_json: Option<String>` with `#[uniffi(default = None)]` (test seam). \n  [...] ## Deviation from the design doc (with justification) \n **Entity-resolve (item 8, "via client.rs:1494") was NOT migrated to OpRunner.** Investigation showed `resolve_nostr_entity` is a `#[uniffi::export(async_runtime = "tokio")]` async method on `HighlighterNmpApp` — it runs on UniFFI's own tokio runtime, **not the kernel actor loop**, so it cannot wedge the single-writer loop and has no `runtime.block_on` actor site. It already carries the 4s `NOSTR_ENTITY_FETCH_TIMEOUT` at the client layer (design §4.1 invariant 3, Class C). Routing it through OpRunner (which feeds `KernelMsg` into the actor) would be architecturally wrong and would break its synchronous-return FFI contract. Left as-is. \n
