0c7b6-172 | 2026-06-12T18:03:56Z | session:0c7b6c09-7d1f-4cb2-b178-1adf69cd09ef | 1. **Deep architectural issue found**: ~92 blocking network awaits inside the NMP actor loop — on a dead network the actor wedges and even my timeout's resolution can't process ("Creating…" hang persists offline). Needs an actor-side audit; too invasive for this session's tail. \n 2. `beta.highlighter.com/api/nip05` returns 404 (server-side route missing) and App Links/universal links need `assetlinks.json`/AASA files deployed. [...] Assistant: **The app — specifically `highlighter-core` (`app/core` in this repo), not the NMP framework.** \n  \n The evidence from the research: \n  \n - **NMP itself is clean and explicit about this.** Its doctrine (D3/D8) forbids blocking the actor thread, and when the framework found the same bug class *inside its own kernel* (V-90), it fixed it with ADR-0040 (accepted 2026-05-31) using a serialized worker + parked-operation pattern. The framework also already ships the right primitives: correlation-ID action results, capability workers, push-based deltas. \n  \n - **The violation is in this repo's facade.** `app/core/src/nmp_app.rs` runs its own single-threaded actor and calls `runtime.block_on(...)` on network-dependent NMP operations *from inside that loop* — waiting up to 360s for a relay ACK (uploads, chat publish, room join) or 65s per signature. Even the timeout constants live here, in `app/core/src/nmp_runtime.rs`, not in NMP. \n  \n - The ironic confirmation: this same app already implements the correct non-blocking pattern in nine bespoke worker threads (account creation, search, bunker sign-in…) — it just didn't apply it consistently to the other ~30 network paths. \n  \n One small caveat: NMP's APIs make the mistake *easy* — `dispatch_nmp_action_for_result` hands you a future that can pend for six minutes, and nothing stops you awaiting it on your actor thread. A framework-side guardrail (e.g. a debug assertion or a callback-style API) could prevent the next app from repeating this. But the defect to fix is in this repo, and the fix (the `OpRunner` design) requires **zero changes to nostr-multi-platform**. [...] **5. Aborted-mid-wait NMP ops leak a `waiters` HashMap entry.** `nmp_runtime.rs:342-352`. When OpRunner aborts an op parked in `wait_for` (`tokio::time::timeout(timeout, rx).await`, line 346), the future is dropped without running the cleanup at 352, so the `waiters` entry persists until a matching row arrives (302) or the process exits. Bounded per user action (not a runaway), and the `completed` cache is LRU-capped (`NMP_ACTION_RESULT_CACHE_LIMIT`, 311), so this is not unbounded accumulation. But it is not zero-leak. A `Drop` guard on the waiter, or pruning stale waiters on insert, would close it. \n
0c7b6-173 | 2026-06-12T18:03:56Z | session:0c7b6c09-7d1f-4cb2-b178-1adf69cd09ef | **1. `RequestJoinRoom` submits a 30s Class-A network op with no busy flag and no pre-submit emit — violates design invariant §4.1(2).** \n `nmp_app.rs:4513-4547`. The success path sets no joining indicator and does not `emit` before `submit_op`. `pending_joins` is runtime-internal and only populated in the apply arm *on success* (`nmp_app.rs:2613-2617`). Between tap and resolution (up to 30s on a slow network) the UI shows nothing; then the room appears or an error toast fires. The design §4.6 specified an acceptance test asserting "the join busy-flag is set" — that assertion was never implemented (`join_room_timeout_resolves_with_live_payload` asserts liveness + eventual error, not a busy flag), so the gap is unguarded. Fix: add an `is_joining` snapshot field set+emitted before submit, cleared in the apply arm; assert it in the join test. \n  [...] **7. `clear_network_action_error` (success path) does not clear `is_saving`.** `nmp_app.rs:6287-6291`. Harmless today only because `apply_op_outcome` unconditionally runs `refresh_network_settings` afterward (`nmp_app.rs:3089`), which sets `is_saving = false` (6193). Latent fragility: if that refresh were ever made conditional, `is_saving` would leak on a successful relay write. Consider clearing it in the success arm directly. \n
0c7b6-174 | 2026-06-12T18:03:56Z | session:0c7b6c09-7d1f-4cb2-b178-1adf69cd09ef | Assistant: The design doc is now at `docs/architecture/actor-blocking-fix.md` (241 lines). The short version: \n  \n **Diagnosis sharpened.** The naive "92 blocking sites" reading was wrong — ~58 are pure local nostrdb reads and fine as-is. The real killers are much more specific: \n - **Class A (5 sites, 360-second waits):** Blossom photo uploads, chat-message publish, and room-join all wait on a relay ACK with a 6-minute protocol timeout — each one freezes the entire actor (and all UI snapshots) for up to six minutes on a dead network. \n - **Class B (~24 sites, 65-second waits):** every publish helper signs through the NMP signer with a 65s timeout — instant with a local key, but up to 65s per signature with a NIP-46 bunker signer. \n - **Class C (3 sites):** bounded 4–6s HTTP fetches; tolerable but serial. \n  \n **The framework already prescribes the answer.** The NMP repo's own doctrine forbids exactly this (D3/D8 non-blocking contracts), and its **ADR-0040 fixed the identical bug class inside nmp-core itself** a couple of weeks ago using a serialized worker + parked-operation pattern. Highlighter's facade violates the doctrine — while ironically already implementing the correct pattern in nine bespoke worker threads (account-create, search, bunker sign-in…). \n  \n **Recommended fix: one `OpRunner` primitive** that generalizes those nine workers — a shared 2-thread tokio runtime, a domain-keyed in-flight registry with generation-based supersession (tap refresh twice → first op aborts) and `AbortHandle` cancellation, a single `KernelMsg::OpResolved` message back to the actor, and all state mutation staying on the actor thread so the exclusive-write-between-emits invariant survives. Timeouts-only was rejected (our 30s account-create timeout already proved insufficient on-device); fully concurrent async handlers were rejected (breaks state consistency). \n  \n **Migration is phased and incremental:** Phase 0 adds stall instrumentation + a `block_on_local` wrapper + a CI lint banning raw `block_on` (1–2 days); Phase 1 converts the top-10 wedge sites including all five Class A killers (4–6 days); Phase 2 sweeps the ~24 publish helpers; Phases 3–4 harden `handle_core_delta` and fold the nine legacy workers into `OpRunner`. It also designs a deterministic dead-network test harness (a `relay_policy_json` config seam — relay URLs are currently compile-time baked — plus a black-hole TCP listener), with an account-creation regression test that fails on today's code by construction. [...] - **CI lint** `/Users/pablofernandez/Work/hl/scripts/lint-actor-blocking.sh`: greps `nmp_app.rs` for `.block_on(` outside the wrapper definition and the allowlist; skips the `#[cfg(test)]` module and comment lines; exits non-zero on violation. Verified it both catches an injected production violation and passes when clean. \n - **CI workflow** `/Users/pablofernandez/Work/hl/.github/workflows/core.yml`: dual-checkout of `hl` + `nostr-multi-platform` (copied from `android.yml`), runs the lint and `cargo test --lib -- --test-threads=1`, with a documented single `|| ` retry tracking the known flake. \n  [...] **Dimension 9 — Residual debt.** Zero TODO/FIXME/HACK introduced. Design doc Status is **"Implemented"** (`docs/architecture/actor-blocking-fix.md:3`) — the prompt's premise that it still says "Proposed" is stale; nothing to flag there. Lint passes on the current tree. The `OpOutcome` match in `apply_op_outcome` is exhaustive (no wildcard arms); every variant is constructed ≥3×. \n  [...] **4. Useless trace tag `"core"` at the StartNostrConnect `block_on_local`.** `nmp_app.rs:4249` — every other of the 80 sites has a descriptive tag (e.g. `"refresh_network_settings"`); this one says `"core"`, which is uninformative in a slow-handler warning. Rename to `"start_nostr_connect"`. \n  \n ### Observations (no action required, but logged for "no debt anywhere")
